  "text_md": "![](<08c9eac4149a44cf8eed7cf1466ce8afa6b7_page_1_pg1_images/imageFile1.png>)\n\nNews\n\noiympic\n\nCamera Club\n\n1452\n\nF\n\nIssue Number 1, 2006\n\n# IMMEDIATE PAST PRESIDENT’S MESSAGE  \n\n![](<08c9eac4149a44cf8eed7cf1466ce8afa6b7_page_1_pg1_images/imageFile2.png>)\n\nDear OPCC members,\n\nMy last five years as a member of OPCC have been very enjoyable and rewarding.\n\nI have met many talented people and have made many friends, and have learned more about photography than I had hoped for.\n\n  The travel bug is still with me having traveled to Europe, Africa, South America and Antarctica since we last met and with a few weeks in Southeast Asia scheduled for February.\n\nI will send Jim my email address once I get settled so we can keep in contact. Thanks again for the friendships and I wish all of you a Great Christmas and all the best in the New Year.\n\nBob Mandich\n\n#   SENSOR DUST  \n\nBob Huelsdonk\n\nLast January; submitted a digital projected competition print from my digital Canon D60 SLR which showed some wind generators against a plain blue sky. Two of our discerning members, Jean Timmermeister and Valerie Henschel commented that the sky had a dim but noticeable dust spot on it. had not noticed it There have been a lot of changes in my life in the last two years, and I have decided to move from western Washington where I have lived all my life to Carlsbad, California. Ihave some family in the area and had met a friend there last year. She is a world traveler and an avid photographer. But the Northwest will always be my \"home\"\n\non my computer screen or even on the projected image until it was pointed out.  \n\nReturning home, I put the print on my computer screen. Indeed it showed as about a 1/2 inch, slightly darker blob (on an 8x10 image). I then increased the contrast greatly, and lo and  \n\n",
